WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.889
It was dark when you arrived at the office.

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:06.980
Rain hammered down on the windowpane, cold
as death.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.720
Your therapist didn’t expect a frantic call
from you in the middle of the night, but he’d

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.790
learned to take such surprises as they came.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.590
You called and drove over the second you had
the dream.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.970
That terrible, terrible dream.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.279
He was sitting at his desk.

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:26.600
When you entered the room, he gestured to
the couch, encouraging you to take a seat

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.650
and make yourself comfortable.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.220
Your heart was beating so hard you worried
it might break your ribs.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.210
Every time you blinked, you were getting flashes
of the faces.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:44.260
Those vacant, grinning faces, with wide eyes
and mouths wrenched open, hanging slack.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:50.090
You hoped to whatever God or Gods you believed
in to never see those images outside of a

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:51.090
dream.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.380
“What’s been troubling you this time?”

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.430
He asked.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.870
“I must admit...I’m getting a little worried
about you.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.350
You seemed so frantic over the phone.”

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.840
You tried your best to settle your breathing
for long enough to speak.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.320
At which point, you said the words you were
hoping you’d never have to say again.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.909
“I had a dream about Cragglewood Park.”

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:13.850
You could almost hear the sigh your therapist
was desperately trying to conceal from you.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:19.380
You’d been making so much progress, processing,
and working to move beyond that mysterious

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.380
trauma.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.560
But then the dreams had come back - Just as
forceful and terrifying as they ever were.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.979
Your therapist asked you to recount it, just
like he did so many times before.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:32.950
But of course, it’s always the same…

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:38.229
In the dream, you were a child - probably
about six or seven - walking down a long,

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:43.609
cobbled path, surrounded on all sides by these
trees that looked like eerie amusement park

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:44.630
animatronics.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:50.149
They had these big, rubbery faces, stretched
into eerie smiles, like something off the

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:52.179
cover of an old children’s book.

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:56.890
The Craggle Trees: You somehow knew, deep
down in your heart, that this was what the

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.469
trees were called.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.709
This was, after all, Cragglewood Park.

00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:07.189
Those words, in whimsical cursive letters,
were written on an aging sign somewhere deep

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:08.399
in your mind.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:11.890
You could remember it as clearly as your own
mother’s face.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.360
And you weren’t alone here in Cragglewood
Park.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.900
There were other children, wandering down
that cobbled road like a lost scene from the

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.160
Wizard of Oz.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.200
The trees grinned and gyrated and sang up
against a painted forest background.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.690
Wait, was it painted or did it just seem somehow
unreal?

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.720
The memories were getting hazy here.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.290
The same goes for the faces of the other children:
They were smudged out; forgotten.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.189
Why were you here?

00:02:38.189 --> 00:02:39.720
Why were any of you here?

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.560
And where were your parents in all this?

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.659
But those thoughts were blotted out by the
singing of those awful trees.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:50.879
You couldn’t remember the words, but the
music was so clear: The haunting notes of

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:55.170
a calliope organ,
like a collapsing memory of a day at the circus.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:03:00.230
You and the other children were all walking
in the same direction, as though in a trance.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.019
What were you walking towards?

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:04.680
The calliope only got louder with every step.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.719
A boy with a smudged-out face to the left
of you tried to speak, but his words were

00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:10.719
muffled.

00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:15.110
You couldn’t hear anything over the music
and the terrible, garbled singing of all of

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:16.700
the animatronic trees.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:22.090
Then there were glowing lights and colors
as you approached the end of the road.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.930
Something big, moving in time to the music.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:31.719
A grand, old carousel, slowly grinding to
a halt to let on the next wave of children.

00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:34.989
You and the others just stood there and watched
it.

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:39.119
Moments later, half of the crowd of assembled
children - the boy standing next to you included

00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:41.700
- began to step onto the carousel.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.269
The light got brighter and the singing got
louder.

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:48.709
By the time you reached the end of recounting
your dream to your therapist, you realized

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:50.659
that your cheeks were wet with tears.

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:54.329
You told him you’d do anything to get the
dreams to stop.

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:55.329
Anything.

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:57.930
Your therapist just nodded and made a note
in his journal.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:02.409
“I’ll get in touch with someone who specializes
in this kind of thing,” he said.

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:04.739
“Try to get some rest in the meantime.

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:08.099
It can be detrimental to obsess about this
kind of thing.”

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:10.120
But how could you not obsess?

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.250
The second you got home, you did what you
always did after having another Cragglewood

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.070
Park dream: You went straight to the forums.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:21.019
After all, the most frightening thing about
the Cragglewood Dream was the fact you weren’t

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:22.759
the only one having it.

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:28.220
A number of people had congregated on a niche
online paranormal forum to discuss their eerily

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.620
similar experiences.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:36.620
Dreams filled with singing uncanny trees,
half-forgotten children, and the hazy horrors

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.000
of Cragglewood Park.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.479
Each member of the forum alerted the others
whenever they had the dream again, and threw

00:04:42.479 --> 00:04:44.930
any new information into the pot.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:50.819
They came from all over the world - different
ages, backgrounds, and countries, making it

00:04:50.819 --> 00:04:55.670
impossible that they’d all actually visited
a place like Cragglewood Park at some point

00:04:55.670 --> 00:05:01.270
in their past - and really, the only thing
they had in common, other than the dream itself,

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:05.160
was the fact they were all only children while
in them.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:10.230
None of them could explain the dream - Perhaps,
with almost eight billion people on earth,

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:16.360
it could be a coincidence that this handful
would have these peculiar dreams.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.710
Perhaps that’s all it was: Strange dreams.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:24.460
A few days later, a stranger arrived at your
door, telling you that they were referred

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:29.910
to you by your therapist: They’d heard about
the dreams, about Cragglewood Park.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:33.930
In fact, they’d dealt with similar cases
in the past, and if you’d be willing to

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:38.970
answer a few questions, they may even have
a viable treatment for the strange dreams

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.919
that have been assailing you for years now.

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:43.660
What could go wrong?

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:48.280
Anything that could stop you from seeing those
awful tree faces would be good…

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.099
You relayed everything that you’d previously
told your therapist.

00:05:52.099 --> 00:05:56.740
Every detail of the dream, the rough number
of times it’d recurred, and even the support

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.940
group for other sufferers that you frequented
online.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.030
After you’d answered all of the stranger’s
questions, they told you that in order to

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:09.270
seek treatment for your strange dreams, you
would need only show up at a nearby building

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:10.909
and tell them who you are.

00:06:10.909 --> 00:06:16.569
They told you that this is a highly confidential
and experimental procedure, so it would need

00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:18.120
to be kept secret.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:22.759
You sign a non-disclosure agreement and happily
submit to all their conditions.

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:26.509
You’ll do anything to stop dreaming about
Cragglewood Park.

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:32.080
Even agree to what you don’t know is Amnestic
Treatment from the SCP Foundation, who are

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.669
testing to see if erasing memories can suppress
the Cragglewood Park dreams.

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:40.550
You were among the first group of guinea pigs
for that experiment.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.350
But hey, if it works, it works, right?

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.030
And for a few weeks, it did.

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:50.460
You submitted yourself to regular Amnestic
Treatment Sessions at a local Foundation front

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.490
business disguised as a legitimate medical
center.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:57.669
You were skeptical at first - you’d tried
every therapy and over-the-counter sleeping

00:06:57.669 --> 00:06:59.979
pill you could get your hands on.

00:06:59.979 --> 00:07:01.669
You tried meditation.

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:02.680
Regression.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.690
Hypnosis.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.270
Positive subliminals.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:09.419
Nothing had banished the haunting dreams of
Cragglewood Park.

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:14.550
Those trees, those faces, those awful, tinny
songs…

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:18.849
But to your immense relief, this new course
of treatment seemed to hold strong.

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:23.120
While taking the medication given to you by
the Foundation, you got your best nights of

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:24.970
sleep in years.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.979
A whole new world of comfort and peace.

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:30.980
But then came a problem: There were supply
issues.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:34.009
You’d need to take a few weeks off the course.

00:07:34.009 --> 00:07:38.650
And just as soon as you stopped taking the
regular course of medications, the second

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:44.190
your head hit the pillow and you drifted off
to sleep, you were right back in Cragglewood

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:45.190
Park.

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:46.190
It wasn’t fair.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:48.120
It just wasn’t fair!

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.919
Perhaps the nightmares would go on forever.

00:07:49.919 --> 00:07:54.180
You’d spend every evening with those other
faceless children, always wondering.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.490
“Who were they?

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:56.940
What happened to them?”

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:01.290
Little did you know, there were no supply
issues, the Foundation just wanted to test

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:06.879
the viability of Amnestic Treatment as a long-term
solution to the Cragglewood Park dreams - or,

00:08:06.879 --> 00:08:10.840
as they dubbed them, SCP-2571.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:16.370
Turns out, the Amnestics were only effective
when the subjects of SCP-2571 were actively

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.770
taking them.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:22.689
Given that Foundation tests have estimated
around 0.05% of the world's population are

00:08:22.689 --> 00:08:29.720
affected by SCP-2571, with that number possibly
on the rise by unknown means, consistent amnestic

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:34.690
treatment for all affected was deemed to not
be a viable solution.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.940
But if it’s any consolation to you, you’re
not suffering alone.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:44.110
The SCP Foundation has an online bot known
as I/O-MANDELA, trawling the internet for

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:47.670
any discussions relevant to SCP-2571.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:53.750
They also have a designated task force, MTF
Phi-7, also known as "The Laughingstock",

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:58.180
to investigate and follow up on cases related
to the sinister Cragglewood Park.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.240
The Foundation has conducted thousands of
interviews related to the subject, but only

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.560
three are appended to the file, containing
between them some revelations on Cragglewood

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:12.290
Park that reveal it’s actually far, far
worse than a childish bad dream.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:18.720
Dr. Reiner, the Research Team Leader for SCP-2571,
conducted the first of the three pertinent

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:25.460
interviews about SCP-2571, investigating the
experience of one Rupert Duccasoux.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:29.690
In many ways, Rupert’s Cragglewood Park
dreams were truly textbook ventures into this

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.860
nightmare of an amusement park.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.750
He described venturing through the park, saying:

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.420
“I enter this theme-park.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:37.610
It's like Disneyland, but smaller.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.420
There's no rides; just this long, winding
road through the woods.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.460
Everything's bright and colorful, like in
a cartoon.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:48.870
And there's these trees all around me, but…They've
all got faces.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:49.870
And they're singing.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.970
They've got these dopey, cheerful looks, like
in one of those old time cartoons, right?

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.930
And they just sing and laugh and sing.”

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:01.530
Like all the other accounts, he reported approaching
a carousel with the other children.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:05.460
Half of the children got on the carousel,
and he and the others began to leave.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.720
At this point, Rupert became visibly distressed
while recounting the tale, and said:

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.520
“Just as I'm getting ready to leave, I see
something.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:14.800
A tiny tree, sprouting up near my foot.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:15.830
It looks up at me.

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:19.970
It's smiling, smiling with that big, dopey,
happy grin.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.430
When I see it, that's when I start screaming.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:23.770
That's when I wake up.”

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:28.160
When Dr. Reiner asked Rupert why he started
screaming, Rupert replied that the tree had

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.570
his face.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:30.570
The interview concluded.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:35.570
Next, Dr. Reiner spoke to Janine Yearling,
another victim of SCP-2571.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:40.310
Dr. Reiner shifted his line of questioning
to the carousel specifically.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:45.050
Ms. Yearling insisted that she never stepped
onto the carousel herself.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:48.140
Some who did were smiling, others were crying.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.470
They hugged some of the children that chose
to stay behind before they got on.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:56.470
The more she described what happened, the
more distressed Ms. Yearling seemed to become.

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:59.350
She eventually broke down into mortified sobs
from the recollection.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.180
When Dr. Reiner asked what was wrong, Ms.
Yearling said:

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:03.460
“One of the kids.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:04.670
One of the kids that got on.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:05.670
Why?

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:06.740
Why did he hug me?

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:09.490
I didn't — I don't even know who he was…”

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:13.610
Before breaking down into horribly desperate,
choked sobbing.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:18.940
The final interview, and what it led to, was
the most eventful and disturbing of all.

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:19.940
Why?

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:25.170
Because this interview, conducted by Dr. Reiner
with Randolph Blair, was the first time that

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:32.070
a physical item in a subject’s home proved
that SCP-2571 may be much more than just a

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:33.920
malicious dream.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:40.110
An old VHS videotape was found in Mr. Blair’s
attic, with the word “CRAGGLEWOOD” scrawled

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:41.250
across the top.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:45.410
When the Foundation made Blair watch the tape
and asked him if he recognized any of the

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:49.800
images in it, he told them that he only recognized
them from nightmares.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:54.360
He theorized that perhaps he’d been shown
the tape as a child, and it had traumatized

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:55.360
him.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.680
The Foundation didn’t think that was the
case.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:02.590
Dr. Reiner pressed on and asked him about
a few strange details: Like the resemblance

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:08.010
between himself and one of the two children
in the video, and the strangely vacant front-facing

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.660
bedroom in his childhood home.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:15.080
At this point, Dr. Reiner realized that Mr.
Blair wouldn’t be forthcoming for much longer.

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:20.460
He decided to use that little time he had
left to ask Mr. Blair one more question: “Have

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.060
you always been an only child?”

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:28.910
That question would forever go unanswered,
but Dr. Reiner could now take an educated

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:29.910
guess.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.380
The Foundation watched and analyzed the tape,
finding it eerily similar to so many of the

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:35.530
dreams that came before.

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:40.900
A young Randolph Blair and another young child
that the Foundation believed to be his brother

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:45.830
wandered through Cragglewood Park with the
others until they finally reached the carousel.

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:50.510
There, Randolph said a tearful goodbye as
his brother stepped up onto the carousel,

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:53.810
knowing somehow that he would never be seen
again.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:58.000
And as Randolph tried to leave in a panic
moments later, he was cornered by another

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:04.950
horrifying Craggle tree, this time with his
brother’s face, singing in his voice.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:08.440
Because Cragglewood Park isn’t a terrifying
dream.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:11.170
It’s a terrifying memory.

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:18.910
Now go check out “Living Ice Cream Van SCP-1386”
and “Waldo Finds You - SCP-4885 - Find Him”

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:22.580
for more anomalies that will transform childhood
joy into heart-stopping terror!

